Title:
NortonAntivirusNextGenEDR

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository describes a Windows endpoint detection and response (EDR) stack implemented with kernel-mode components plus user-mode sensors and rule engines. It aims to provide enterprise-style telemetry and detection (process/memory, filesystem, network, ETW, AMSI) and includes anti-tamper/hook-detection logic to spot common EDR evasion. Notable advanced features include an optional Intel VT-x/EPT hypervisor for memory monitoring and deception, and an optional ELAM boot-start driver for early boot driver classification/blocking (e.g., LOLDrivers). It also implements adversary deception by poisoning credential reads (including LSASS read-buffer patching and EPT shadow-page tricks) rather than simply blocking access. The project integrates YARA, Sigma-like rules, and capa for capability tagging, with JSONL logging, a local REST API, and Elasticsearch ECS forwarding. Useful primarily for defenders/researchers building or studying EDR internals, and for red teams to understand detection surfaces and deception-based countermeasures.

Technical Focus:
- Intel VT-x hypervisor, VM-exits, EPT permissioning/split pages, MTF-based shadow paging
- Windows kernel drivers: WDM, minifilter (FltMgr), WFP callouts, kernel callbacks
- Syscall/alt-syscall interception, SSDT/inline/EAT/ETW hook detection
- AMSI provider + AMSI bypass patch detection
- ETW provider/consumers (including Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence), Sysmon/SACL enrichment
- Rule-based detection pipelines (YARA, Sigma-lite, capa) and ECS/Elasticsearch shipping

Use Cases:
- Study/reference implementation for Windows EDR architecture (kernel + user-mode)
- Detect process injection, hollowing/ghosting, RWX staging, reflective loading, and hook-based evasion
- Monitor/alert on ransomware-like filesystem behavior via minifilter heuristics
- Network egress monitoring and port-based blocking with PID attribution (WFP)
- Early-boot driver vetting/blocking via ELAM (e.g., LOLDriver prevention)
- Research deception techniques against credential theft (LSASS read poisoning, decoy artifacts)

Keywords:
Windows kernel driver, EDR, Intel VT-x, EPT, VMX, VM-exit, Monitor Trap Flag, ELAM, IoRegisterBootDriverCallback, minifilter, FltRegisterFilter, WFP, FwpsCalloutRegister, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, AMSI, IAntimalwareProvider, SSDT, inline hook detection, LOLDrivers, LSASS, NtReadVirtualMemory, process injection, YARA, Sigma, capa, Sysmon, Elasticsearch ECS